VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: T14
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Facts verified independently (not just transcribed from the two verdicts):
- go build ./... and go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... -run TaxOptimizer
  both pass on the actual worktree.
- Diff is confined to web/templates/components/whatif/tax-optimizer.html
  (16 +/-) and the new internal/handlers/whatif/tax_optimizer_render_test.go,
  matching .swarm/manifests/T14.2.files exactly; the other modified paths in
  git status are unrelated in-flight work (classifier/*, dataloader/*,
  transaction.go, ledger.tsv).
- Reproduced probe (a) myself on a scratch copy (sha256
  eda62ab0...feae07a0 confirmed pristine before/after): deleting the two new
  tbody <td> cells causes go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... to FAIL, as
  required.
- Reproduced the swap probe myself: swapping only the inner expressions of
  the Peak Rate <td> (line 99) and Roth Conv <td> (line 100) — so
  TotalRothConverted's formatMoney output lands under the "Peak Rate" header
  and PeakMarginalBracket's percent output lands under "Roth Conv" — leaves
  ALL FOUR tests passing. This is a real, reproducible gap: the tbody
  assertions are strings.Contains against the whole scoped tbody, not
  positional/per-cell, so a column-identity regression ships silently.
  checker-second's factual claim is correct; checker-tests' PASS did not
  contest this fact, it simply wasn't in scope of what checker-tests
  evaluated against.

The two lanes therefore do not actually disagree on any fact. They disagree
on whether the written standard for this attempt required this probe to be
covered. That is the question this ruling turns on, and it must be answered
from the text of "Written acceptance criteria for attempt 2," which is the
sole governing document cited for this dispute (no SPEC.md/ACCESSIBILITY.md
section is cited against T14 — this is a code-test-binding dispute, not a
content/a11y one).

Criterion 4 reads: "Tests must BIND — five enumerated mutation probes must
fail: (a) ... (e) .... Body assertions scoped to tbody, header assertions to
thead, colspan test counts real <th>s against the parsed colspan value."

Read as written, the em-dash and colon introduce an operational definition,
not an illustrative minimum. "Tests must bind" is immediately unpacked into
a closed, lettered list (a)-(e) plus three named scoping/counting mechanics
(tbody scoping, thead scoping, colspan-vs-parsed-th-count). Nothing in the
sentence says "at least," "including," "such as," or "and equivalent
mutations" — the phrasing one would expect if the enumeration were meant as
representative rather than exhaustive. Compare to criterion 3, which is
open ("Zero renders '—' in headline + body for both fields; glossary notes
the dash") and is satisfied by whatever renders correctly, not by a fixed
list — criterion 4's drafters clearly knew how to write an open requirement
and chose not to here. That this is "attempt 2" reinforces the reading: the
enumerated list is almost certainly the direct, deliberately narrowed
response to attempt 1's specific vacuity failures (unscoped assertions,
first-close truncation on nested tbody, "$0.00" collision) — precisely the
failure modes (a)-(e) target. A column-swap regression is a different bug
class (positional/identity, not presence/deletion/scope) that was not among
those failure modes and was not added to the list for attempt 2.

Independently, I verified all five enumerated probes are satisfied (probe
(a) directly by me; (b)-(e) via checker-tests' cited before/after test
output and file line references, which I read against the actual template
and test file and found consistent with the source — e.g., thead/tbody
extraction functions at lines 95-127, colspan literals at line 112 matching
the 7/9 counted in tests, zero-dash guards at lines 28-29/99-100 matching
the `eq $x 0.0` pattern). The scoping/counting mechanics named in criterion
4 (tbody scoping, thead scoping, colspan-vs-real-<th>-count) are also
implemented exactly as specified.

checker-second's charter (CLAUDE.md: "asks 'what would make this wrong,'
defaults to FAIL on ambiguity") governs why an adversarial lane exists and
justifies probing beyond the four probes it re-ran from attempt 1 — but it
does not relax the specific, closed acceptance text governing this
particular attempt when that text is unambiguous. There is no ambiguity
here to default against: criterion 4 enumerates exactly what "bind" means
for this task, and the work meets it letter-for-letter. The swap defect is
real, worth recording, and should become an explicit probe (f) — or a new
task — for a future attempt; it is not, on the text as written, grounds to
fail attempt 2.

Ruling: OVERRULE. checker-second's FAIL is set aside; attempt 2 meets the
written acceptance criteria. Recommend the lead record the swap-coverage gap
as a follow-up item (e.g., an added probe (f) in a future SPEC.md ruling or
a new lightweight task) rather than reopening T14 attempt 2.
